course: medicine
lesson: 8
title: Fever
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A fever is your body turning up its own heating to help fight an infection; normal is about 36.5 to 37.5 degrees.
explain: You shiver while your body is heating up and sweat when it cools down, which is why a fever feels boiling then freezing.
explain: The fever is not the illness, it is the body fighting; medicine like paracetamol lowers it for a few hours but doesn't kill the germ, so it comes back until you've won.
explain: Don't bundle up under blankets to sweat it out: light clothes, a cool room and plenty to drink are best.
explain: A rash of tiny spots that doesn't fade when you press a glass on it, or a child who is floppy and very sleepy, is an emergency.
fact: A fever is when the body raises its temperature to fight infection; normal body temperature is about 36.5 to 37.5 degrees Celsius.
fact: Shivering happens while the body heats up, and sweating while it cools down.
fact: Bundling a feverish child in blankets can make them overheat; light clothes and a cool room are better, and cold baths are not advised either.
fact: With a fever the body loses water by sweating and fast breathing; not drinking is more worrying than not eating, so doctors ask about drinking and weeing to check the body has enough water.
fact: At home children's temperature is measured with a digital thermometer in the armpit or ear; a hand on the forehead is not reliable.
fact: A high temperature can make vivid dreams and odd feelings that go when the fever drops.
fact: Febrile seizures, or fever fits, are fits caused by a rising fever in children aged about 6 months to 5 years; they are scary but almost always harmless and don't mean epilepsy, an illness of repeated fits.
fact: Fever medicines lower the temperature for a few hours but don't kill the infection, so the fever can come back.
fact: A rash that doesn't fade under a pressed glass, with fever, can mean meningitis or sepsis, very dangerous infections, and needs 999 or A&E straight away.
fact: Children usually stay off school until the fever has gone without medicine hiding it.
fact: Paracetamol and ibuprofen are both safe for most children at the right dose; aspirin is not given to children under 16.
exercise: What is a fever? | The body raising its temperature to fight infection | A disease you catch from spending too long in hot weather | The body getting too cold inside | A kind of red rash on the skin
exercise: What is normal body temperature? | About 36.5 to 37.5 degrees | About 39 to 40 degrees Celsius | About 30 degrees, like a cool day | About 100 degrees, like boiling
exercise: Why do you shiver at the start of a fever? | The body is heating itself up | It means you are already getting better | The room around you is too hot | The germs inside you are cold and shaking
exercise: Should you sweat a fever out under lots of blankets? | No, light clothes and a cool room are better | Yes, the more blankets the better, to sweat the germs out | Yes, and a hot bath straight after | No, take a freezing ice bath instead
exercise: Why does the doctor ask if you are drinking and weeing? | To check you have enough water in your body | To see if you like juice better than plain water | To test how good your memory is | Because doctors are just nosy
exercise: How should a grown-up check a child's temperature? | With a digital thermometer | With a cool hand on the forehead | By looking at how red the cheeks are | By asking how hot you feel
exercise: Why do people have strange dreams with a fever? | A high temperature muddles the brain for a while | They are slowly going mad from the heat | The medicine has magic in it that makes pictures | The germs show them pictures at night
exercise: What is a febrile seizure? | A fit caused by a fever rising fast in a young child | A sure sign that the child will have epilepsy for life | Brain damage caused by too much heat | A kind of hiccup that comes with a cold
exercise: Why can a fever come back after the medicine? | The medicine lowers the temperature but doesn't kill the germ | The medicine was fake and never did anything at all | The medicine made a brand new fever | The thermometer is broken
exercise: What does it mean if a rash doesn't fade under a pressed glass? | It could be serious, so get emergency help | It is just a heat rash from the blankets | It means the fever is over and you're well | Nothing, all rashes are the same
exercise: When can you usually go back to school after a fever? | When the fever has gone without medicine hiding it | As soon as you have taken a tablet that morning | The same afternoon if you feel bored | After a whole month at home
exercise: Which medicine is NOT given to children under 16? | Aspirin | Paracetamol | Ibuprofen at the right dose | Children's fever syrup
exercise: Is a fever itself the illness? | No, it is the body fighting the infection | Yes, the fever is the germ itself | Yes, and it must be stopped at once, whatever it takes | No, it is just the weather
exercise: What is more worrying in a sick child? | Not drinking | Not being very hungry | Wanting to sleep a bit more | Wanting to watch cartoons
test: 38.5 and I'm freezing, mum, gimme all the duvets! Good idea? | No, too many covers can make you overheat | Yes, sweating it out under the lot is best | Yes, and a hot water bottle on your head | No, go sit in a cold bath
test: Gran felt my forehead and says I don't have a fever. Can she be sure? | No, touch isn't reliable; a thermometer is | Yes, grans' hands are always accurate | Yes, foreheads never lie | No, only a doctor can feel a fever with his hand
test: My baby cousin had a fit with a fever. Does she have epilepsy now? | No, febrile seizures don't mean epilepsy | Yes, every fit means epilepsy for life | Yes, and brain damage too | No, babies can't have fits at all
test: The fever came back, so the paracetamol was useless. Right? | No, it lowers the fever for hours while the body fights | Yes, it never works on a real fever | Yes, so take a double dose now | No, so give her aspirin instead, it's much stronger stuff
test: Ellie has a fever, is very sleepy and floppy, and has purple spots that stay when a glass is pressed. What now? | Call 999 or go straight to A&E | Let her sleep and wait until the morning | Give her a cold bath | Send her to school as normal
test: Your friend's mum says ibuprofen is dangerous for kids. What's true? | At the right dose it's safe for most children, though some shouldn't have it | It is deadly for every child | It is safer than water, so give as much as you like | Children should take adult doses
test: In a cartoon, a sick boy's thermometer shoots out the top and he's sent to school. What's wrong? | A child with a fever should stay home | Thermometers can't really go that high | Fevers mean you must run it off outside | Nothing, fevers don't matter at all at school
test: What would happen if your body couldn't raise its temperature? | Fighting some infections would be harder | You would never get ill again | You'd be cured faster | Nothing would change at all
test: Dylan saw spiders on the ceiling when his fever was high. Is he going mad? | No, fever can cause odd feelings that pass when it drops | Yes, it's a sign of madness | Yes, the spiders are real ones | No, he is just making it up for attention
test: Ill, not hungry at all, but sipping water fine. Should Mum worry about the food? | Not much; not eating is normal, drinking matters more | Yes, she should make him eat a big meal right now | Yes, call 999 | No, but stop the water too
test: Why do you sweat when a fever breaks? | The body is cooling down again | The germs are leaking out through the skin | You are getting hotter and hotter still | The heavy blankets made your skin wet
exam: Identify the normal range of human body temperature. | Approximately 36.5 to 37.5 degrees Celsius | Approximately 39 to 40 degrees Celsius in health | Approximately 30 to 31 degrees Celsius | Approximately 45 degrees Celsius
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises fever? | A deliberate elevation of temperature by which the body combats infection | A disease arising from prolonged exposure to warm weather | A condition requiring immediate immersion of the patient in iced water | An infection in its own right, independent of any organism
exam: It is given that a febrile child presents a rash which does not fade under the pressure of a glass. State the appropriate course of action. | Emergency medical assistance is to be obtained immediately | The child is to be observed at home until the following week | The child is to be sent to school | The rash is to be treated with moisturiser
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the administration of aspirin. | Aspirin is not to be given to children under the age of sixteen | Aspirin is the preferred remedy for fever in children | Aspirin may be given at an adult dose to any child of school age | Aspirin is safer than paracetamol in childhood
